-Two approaches remain credible:
+Validation rejects a submission whose field types are invalid, whose `plot_id` is not a known catalog plot, whose `base_revision_id` is unknown or belongs to another visit, or that omits `base_revision_id` while the visit already has revisions. A visit's first accepted submission has no base and becomes the root revision.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Idempotency keys are scoped to the author. The record store retains the normalized submission envelope for each accepted key. Two submissions are identical when their visit ID, base revision ID, and all visit payload fields (`plot_id`, `observation_date`, `observers`, `species_cover_estimates`, `notes`) match after normalization by the visit schema.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Accepting Submissions
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Acceptance is atomic per visit, so concurrent submissions cannot both advance the current revision from the same base without one becoming a competing candidate.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+When the author's idempotency key has already been accepted:
+
+- An identical envelope returns the original acceptance result verbatim, including the acceptance-time current revision, unresolved flag, and candidate set, without creating another revision. A caller that needs the visit's live state performs a current-state retrieval.
+- A different envelope is rejected and leaves the original submission and revisions unchanged.
+
+For a new key and a valid envelope, the record store creates the revision with the supplied base as its parent. Any known revision of the visit is a valid base, including a non-leaf ancestor or a dismissed revision; each such submission creates a new leaf in the revision tree. If the base equals the current revision, the new revision becomes the current revision. If the base is any other revision, the current revision stays unchanged and the new revision is a competing candidate.
+
+A stale base is therefore accepted rather than rejected: the submission is preserved in full, and the divergence enters review. No submission is discarded because another worker edited first.
+
+The acceptance result contains the submission ID, revision ID, current revision ID, unresolved flag, and the current candidate revision IDs.
+
+## Revision Graph, Current Revision, and Unresolved Work
+
+- A **leaf revision** is a revision that no accepted revision names as its parent.
+- A **dismissed revision** is a revision that appeared in a resolution's reviewed set without being selected. Dismissed revisions remain in history and are no longer candidates.
+- A **candidate revision** is an undismissed leaf revision.
+- The **current revision** always names a candidate leaf. It is the revision a new edit should use as its base to extend the visit without creating a competing branch.
+- A visit is **unresolved** when it has more than one candidate revision.
+
+A linear submission based on the current revision advances the current revision and keeps the visit resolved if it was already resolved. Any submission based on another revision creates a new candidate and leaves the visit unresolved until a coordinator acts. Extending a dismissed branch creates a new, undismissed candidate and reopens review; the dismissed revision itself remains dismissed.
+
+While a visit is unresolved, its current revision is the operational base for new edits but is not a scientifically authoritative record. Consumers that need an authoritative visit state must check the unresolved flag rather than treating the current revision as final.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+The current-state response contains:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | Visit UUID |
+| `current_revision_id` | Revision a new edit should use as its base |
+| `current_payload` | Complete payload of the current revision |
+| `unresolved` | True when more than one candidate revision exists |
+| `candidate_revision_ids` | All candidate revisions, including the current revision |
+
+The current-state, revision-history, list-unresolved, and resolve operations return `not_found` when `visit_id` has no accepted revision. A submission is never `not_found`: it creates the visit's root revision when the envelope omits a base.
+
+The revision-history response returns a flat list of every revision ordered by server receipt time ascending. Each entry carries its parent revision ID, submission ID, author ID, receipt time, payload, the original submission envelope, and any resolution in which it was considered or selected. The parent IDs reconstruct the revision tree. Reviewers compare complete candidate payloads from this history; the list of unresolved visits supplies the review queue.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A resolution selects one complete revision as the visit's current revision. It does not merge, edit, or combine revision payloads. A coordinator who needs a corrected record submits a new complete revision like any other author.
+
+The resolution request contains:
+
+| Field | Required | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | yes | Visit being resolved |
+| `selected_revision_id` | yes | Candidate revision chosen as current |
+| `reviewed_revision_ids` | yes | Candidate revisions the coordinator compared |
+| `idempotency_key` | yes | Client-generated key identifying one logical resolution attempt |
+
+Resolution is atomic per visit and serialized with submission acceptance for that visit, so a candidate cannot be accepted between the reviewed-set check and the dismissal. Two concurrent resolutions cannot both succeed against the same candidate set.
+
+The selected revision must appear in the reviewed set, and the reviewed set must exactly equal the visit's current candidate revisions. Validation order is: idempotency, unresolved state, reviewed-set match, then selected membership. An identical retry of an accepted resolution key returns the original resolution result before any live candidate check, even if later submissions have since created new candidates. Reusing the key with a different request is rejected as an idempotency conflict without changing state. A visit with fewer than two candidate revisions is rejected as `not_unresolved`. A reviewed set that differs from the live candidate set is rejected as `stale_review_set`, with the current candidate set returned and no state changes; this prevents a coordinator's decision from dismissing a revision they never saw. A selected revision absent from the reviewed set is rejected as `invalid_selection`.
+
+On acceptance, the record store records the coordinator ID, selected revision, reviewed set, and resolution time; dismisses the reviewed revisions other than the selected one; and makes the selected revision current. The visit is resolved immediately because every candidate was either selected or dismissed.
+
+Resolution idempotency keys are scoped to the coordinator. Repeating a key with an identical request returns the original resolution result. Reusing it with a different request is rejected without changing state.
+
+## Invariants
+
+- Revisions are immutable and are never deleted, overwritten, or combined.
+- Every revision belongs to exactly one visit, and every non-root revision has a parent in the same visit.
+- A visit has exactly one root revision.
+- The current revision is always a candidate leaf.
+- A visit is unresolved exactly when it has more than one candidate revision.
+- A resolution never changes a revision payload.
+- A visit is export-eligible only when `unresolved` is false. The record store supplies the signal; review-export owns the export set and CSV shape.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: each submission is a complete visit derived from a named base revision; divergent submissions become competing candidates | Field-aware three-way merge; last-write-wins; rejecting stale submissions | Preserves each worker's visit as a scientific unit. Avoids merge rules that can combine related edits, such as a species identification and its cover estimate, without understanding the relationship. Accepts a larger review queue in exchange for simpler, more inspectable behavior. |
+| Revision identity | Server assigns immutable revision IDs; submissions name a base revision ID | Client-generated revision IDs; base identified by submission ID | Gives tablets a stable pointer for later submissions and idempotent retries while keeping identity allocation under server control. |
+| Stale base handling | Accept the submission as a competing revision | Reject the submission and require a rebase; auto-rebase onto current | Preserves the submitted edit and its original scientific context. Rejection would force a worker to re-enter observations against a state they may not have seen. |
+| Current state during review | Return the current revision and payload with an explicit unresolved flag and the candidate set | Hide the payload until resolved; return no current revision | Tablet-sync can continue operating and explain the pending state. Review-export can exclude the visit by checking the flag. |
+| Resolution action | Select one existing candidate revision | Merge candidates field by field at review time; accept all candidates | Keeps the whole-visit unit and avoids reintroducing merge semantics in the review workflow. Correction after review remains an ordinary new submission. |
+| Resolution concurrency | Require the coordinator's reviewed candidate set to match the live candidate set exactly | Resolve whichever revision is selected; dismiss only listed revisions | Prevents a newly arrived candidate from being dismissed unseen. |
+| Resolution retries | Idempotency key scoped to the coordinator | No retry protection; rely on selected revision alone | A retried resolution cannot create a second outcome or dismiss a different set. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ The concurrency policy is whole-visit revisions rather than field-aware three-way merge.
+2. ✅ Stale-base submissions become competing candidates instead of being rejected.
+3. ✅ Resolution selects one complete revision; the record store never combines revision payloads.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Recovery when two tablets submit a first revision for the same `visit_id` with no base: the second submission is rejected because the visit already has revisions. The recovery flow belongs to tablet-sync, which must fetch the existing revision and resubmit against it.
+2. Revision-history retention beyond the first release. No deletion or compaction policy is defined yet.
+3. The source of coordinator-role membership. The record store enforces the role on resolution, but role assignment remains with the team's identity provider.
+4. Whether a visit's `plot_id` may be corrected between revisions. The record store validates the referenced plot but treats the field as part of the whole-visit payload; review compares any resulting candidates.
